---
// Case study for poolproof.
//
// Facts were checked by the poolproof session against the repo (main fcb058d),
// GitHub and crates.io on 2026-11-22. Rules for this page:
// - Name only bugs that are public upstream. Unreported findings stay off it,
//   including the pooler that allowed one connection over its pool size.
// - The upstream issues were filed by other people; poolproof reproduces them.
//   Nihal's part in pgdog#1849 is the cause analysis and fix branch, not merged.
// - No adoption claims. Supavisor 2.10.0-rc.1 is a release candidate.
// - No em dashes in the copy.
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Matrix from "../../components/Matrix.astro";
import SharedBackend from "../../components/cs/SharedBackend.astro";
import data from "../../data/poolproof.json";

const { trace, counts, scenarios } = data;
const targets = data.groups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.targets.length, 0);
const sql = (action: string) => action.replace(/^[a-z]+:\s*/, "");
const pooler = trace.target.split(" ")[0];

// The trace as lines of text, each with the class that colours it
const lines = trace.steps.flatMap((step, i) => {
  const out = [
    { cls: "is-dim", text: `${i + 1}  ${step.actor.padEnd(15)}` },
    { cls: "", text: `${sql(step.action)}\n` },
  ];
  if (step.differs) {
    out.push(
      { cls: "is-dim", text: "   Postgres       " },
      { cls: "is-good", text: `${step.postgres}\n` },
      { cls: "is-dim", text: `   ${pooler.padEnd(15)}` },
      { cls: "is-bad", text: `${step.pooler}\n` },
    );
  } else {
    out.push({ cls: "is-dim", text: "   both           " }, { cls: "", text: `${step.postgres}\n` });
  }
  return out;
});

// as in poolproof's docs/scenarios.md
const SCENARIO = `send = ["parse s1 SELECT 'a'::text", "bind - s1", "execute -", "sync"]`;
---

<CaseStudy
  title="What a connection pooler changes"
  dek="Poolers are meant to be invisible to the application. poolproof checks that claim: it runs the same small scenarios against Postgres and through a pooler, and reports every step where the answers differ. Most of the work went into making sure a pass actually means something."
  source={{ label: "The repository on GitHub", url: "https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof" }}
  meta={[
    { text: "Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof", href: "https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof" },
    { text: "v0.1.1, 4 Nov 2026" },
    { text: "Rust" },
    { text: "MIT OR Apache-2.0" },
    { text: "results site", href: data.source },
  ]}
  next={{ title: "Wrong rows from a self-join", href: "/work/joinscan-wrong-rows" }}
>
  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      PgBouncer, PgDog, Supavisor, PgCat and Odyssey sit between an application and Postgres and share a few server
      connections among many clients. In transaction mode a client holds a server connection only for the length of a
      transaction, so anything that lives on the connection instead of in the transaction is at risk: a
      <code>SET</code>, a temp table, a prepared statement, an advisory lock.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most poolers document some of this. There was no way to check across poolers and versions which of these
      actually break and how.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Postgres as the oracle</h2>
    <p>
      A poolproof scenario is a short script of steps from one or more clients. It runs twice: once against Postgres
      directly, once through the pooler, and every step's result is compared. Postgres is the reference, so no
      scenario hardcodes the answer it expects.
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term">{lines.map((line) => (
        <span class:list={[line.cls]}>{line.text}</span>
      ))}</div>
      <figcaption>
        multi-statement-begin through {trace.target}. Postgres runs all three statements; through {pooler} the client
        gets back only the BEGIN, and a direct check afterwards finds no row.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>A difference is not just a failure. What matters is how the application finds out:</p>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>Silent</strong>: the pooler answers, and the answer is wrong.</li>
      <li><strong>Error</strong>: the pooler refuses, so at least the application knows.</li>
      <li><strong>Hang</strong>: no reply at all, until something times out.</li>
      <li><strong>Documented</strong>: a failure the pooler's own docs describe. Still a failure, but not a bug.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>A pass that meant nothing</h2>
    <p>
      The first version compared the two runs step by step and called it a pass when they matched. That turned out to
      be weaker than it looked. To test whether a <code>SET</code> leaks from one client to another, the two clients
      have to land on the same server connection. In transaction mode they may simply never do so, and then "no leak"
      only means "never shared".
    </p>
    <p>
      So steps can now be marked as evidence. Through the pooler they read the backend's process id; on Postgres
      directly, its start time. A run counts only if the clients really did share a backend. If no run does, the
      result is not a pass but a skip, with the reason spelled out: the second client never reached the first one's
      server connection.
    </p>
    <SharedBackend />
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Checking the pool before trusting it</h2>
    <p>
      Before any scenario runs, poolproof probes the pool's real shape: when a second client arrives while the first is
      busy, does it wait, get its own backend, or get refused? Scenarios that need exactly one server connection skip
      when the pool turns out to have several, so a result is never trusted on a pool that isn't the shape its
      configuration claims.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>A failure blamed on the wrong pooler</h2>
    <p>
      With several poolers in one test bed, one pooler's leaked advisory lock (a backend that kept it after its client
      left) made a scenario on a different pooler hang, and the failure looked like that pooler's bug.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fix is a reset before each scenario that ends only the target pooler's backends, matched by process id,
      start time, user and address, and then waits for the pooler to answer again. Later a CI run still found a
      disconnected client's lock held, because a backend exits asynchronously; the check now waits for those backends
      to be gone before reading their state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Results that flip</h2>
    <p>
      Some results are timing-sensitive and change between runs. Retrying until they pass would hide exactly the
      behaviour worth reporting. Instead a scenario can run several times, fails if any run fails, and reports how many
      did. A known-flaky list covers the rest, each entry as narrow as possible and citing the CI run that showed it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Documented is a claim that needs a source</h2>
    <p>
      A failure is marked documented only when an entry quotes the pooler's own documentation word for word, at a
      pinned commit. A test checks every entry against the committed results, so a stale entry fails CI. And a bug
      that has been reported upstream is never marked documented, even when the docs half cover it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Speaking the protocol directly</h2>
    <p>
      Some bugs live below SQL. The Rust client poolproof started on gives every prepared statement a globally unique
      name, which made it impossible to test two clients using the same statement name. So scenarios can also send
      raw wire-protocol messages through a small client of its own, with SCRAM and MD5 authentication:
    </p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term">{SCENARIO}</div>
    </figure>
    <p>That is how it tests Flush, pipelining, COPY and statement-name collisions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The results</h2>
    <p>
      The grid below is Postgres {data.postgres}: {scenarios.length} scenarios across {targets} pooler versions and
      configurations, 13 core targets plus 11 variants (session mode, a pool of four, non-default settings) that run
      nightly. {counts.pass} pass, {counts.silent} fail silently, {counts.errorOrHang} error or hang, and
      {" "}{counts.expected} are documented limitations. The core targets also run on Postgres 17.11. Select a cell to see what
      it checks and where the answers split.
    </p>
    <div class="cs-wide">
      <Matrix />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What it found</h2>
    <p>
      The scenarios are built from 14 upstream issues. Some reproduce on the versions tested; others show a fix
      holding. Four of the clearest:
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>
        PgDog 0.1.50 to 0.1.62 answers only the <code>BEGIN</code> of <code>BEGIN; INSERT; COMMIT</code> sent as one
        string, and never commits the insert if the client disconnects
        (<a href="https://github.com/pgdogdev/pgdog/issues/1849" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">pgdog#1849</a>,
        reported by ewhauser).
      </li>
      <li>
        Supavisor 2.9.13 hangs on four pipelined requests in 9 of 10 runs
        (<a href="https://github.com/supabase/supavisor/issues/1239" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">supavisor#1239</a>,
        reported by richardwattie). The 2.10.0 release candidate answers them.
      </li>
      <li>
        PgBouncer 1.26.1 on Postgres 18 drops a client that set an empty <code>search_path</code> with
        <code>set_config()</code>, once another client has changed it
        (<a href="https://github.com/pgbouncer/pgbouncer/issues/1663" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">pgbouncer#1663</a>,
        reported by AKutlakhmetov).
      </li>
      <li>
        Every pooler but Odyssey, with default settings, keeps a session advisory lock after the client that took it
        disconnects
        (<a href="https://github.com/pgdogdev/pgdog/issues/1407" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">pgdog#1407</a>,
        reported by mhenrixon). PgBouncer, PgDog, PgCat and Supavisor document this one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>From a red cell to a fix</h2>
    <p>
      The PgDog bug shows why silent failures matter. On the issue, a team reported running a migration through PgDog
      as one multi-statement transaction and disconnecting. The client saw success. Neither the
      <code>ALTER TABLE</code> nor the <code>UPDATE</code> ran.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cause is in how PgDog routes a multi-statement query on a single shard: by its first statement only. When
      that statement is <code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>, PgDog answers it itself, and
      the rest of the string is either replayed later with its results thrown away, or dropped.
    </p>
    <p>
      PgDog currently takes outside fixes as linked branches rather than pull requests, so I posted the cause on the issue with a
      branch: in that case the whole string goes to the server unchanged, which is what already happens with PgDog's
      query parser off. It has a unit test and an integration test that fails on main
      (<a href="https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/pgdog/tree/fix/multi-stmt-begin" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">fix/multi-stmt-begin</a>).
      It is not merged.
    </p>
    <p>
      Running poolproof against the branch and its parent, multi-statement-begin goes from fail to pass on Postgres 17
      and 18, and nothing else regresses. The related shapes pass on the branch too: <code>COMMIT</code> first, a
      <code>BEGIN</code> left open, other spellings of <code>BEGIN</code>, and the extended-protocol shape.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What is still open</h2>
    <p>
      Two shapes still fail on the branch, for a separate reason. And the branch's own test expects
      <code>SELECT 1</code> to succeed after the aborted string, where Postgres returns <code>25P02</code>: the test
      encodes the pooler's behaviour, not Postgres's. Exactly the kind of difference poolproof exists to catch.
    </p>
    <p>
      RDS Proxy and hosted poolers are not in the test bed yet; the docs have recipes for testing them by hand.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .cs-wide {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
